# Line ending policy
#
# This tree is ~76% CRLF (1665 files) and ~24% LF (527), because it began as a
# Windows codebase and the Linux work since has added LF files. Upstream
# (github.com/openSYDE/openSYDE) is CRLF throughout, and this fork still merges
# releases from it.
#
# Policy: git does NOT convert line endings. Files keep exactly the bytes they
# have. This is deliberate.
#
# `text=auto` was tried and rejected: it renormalises every file the moment git
# touches it, which turned a 45-file repair into a 30052-line rewrite. Doing
# that across all 2192 files would poison `git blame` for the whole codebase and
# conflict with every in-flight branch and every future upstream merge, since
# upstream's blobs stay CRLF.
#
# `-text` also makes behaviour identical for everyone regardless of their
# personal core.autocrlf setting, which is the other way this used to bite.
#
# What this does NOT do is stop editing tools from injecting the wrong ending
# into a file. That is a tooling discipline problem: read and write with the
# file's existing endings preserved (in Python, open with newline=""). 52 files
# had been damaged that way and were repaired in the commit that added this file.
#
# If a full normalisation is ever wanted, do it as one deliberate commit and use
#     git merge -Xrenormalize upstream/master
# for subsequent upstream merges.

* -text

# Binaries: never touch, and never try to diff as text.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.a      binary
*.so     binary
*.dll    binary
*.exe    binary
*.lib    binary
*.chm    binary
*.syde   binary

# Firmware images are records of what was flashed; keep them byte exact.
*.hex    binary
